      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that both chosen members are girls from a club of 10 members (5 boys and 5 girls), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10:**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 members from 10 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\n   Here, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 45 ways to choose 2 members out of 10.\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5:**\n\n   Similarly, the number of ways to choose 2 girls from 5 is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 10 ways to choose 2 girls out of 5.\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that both chosen members are girls:**\n\n   The probability \\( P \\) that both chosen members are girls is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes:\n\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{both girls}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the fraction:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both chosen members are girls is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).",
